WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.460
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.770
I'm Dustin Kirkland, a
product manager at Google,

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.680
and your host at KubeCon
Copenhagen. I'm joined here

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.870
by one of my colleagues,
Matt Rickard,

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.530
who's a software engineer on
Google's container tools team.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.110
Matt, tell us a little
bit about scaffold.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:17.980
MATT RICKARD: Sure.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.360
Skaffold is a developer
workflow tool made specifically

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.592
for Kubernetes.

00:00:23.592 --> 00:00:25.300
It helps you get
started with Kubernetes.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.670
It helps you develop
your apps on Kubernetes.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.460
And it really targets that
inner development workflow

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.619
of build and deploy.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.020
And it also utilizes
some of the other tools

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.940
that we've published on our
team, such as Kaniko, which

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.430
is are on-cluster build system.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.150
So the idea is that you can
use Skaffold to not only build

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.790
and deploy, but with your
cluster as your only kind

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:46.452
of dependency.

00:00:46.452 --> 00:00:47.410
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.920
And Skaffold interfaces
with other aspects

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.129
of Google Kubernetes or
Kubernetes in general?

00:00:54.129 --> 00:00:54.920
MATT RICKARD: Yeah.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.960
So the Kubernetes API is
exactly what we're targeting.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.410
So with Skaffold, it's
pluggable in the sense

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.629
that you can build and
deploy in a variety of ways,

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:07.030
but your cluster is really
the only thing that you need.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:07.863
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:01:07.863 --> 00:01:11.650
So a developer writing
some code on their MacBook,

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:14.230
walk us through, I
just wrote some Ruby

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.870
and I want to
deploy that to GKE.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:17.920
MATT RICKARD: Sure.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.310
So you write your code, you
write your Dockerfile, which

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.490
is your way to describe
your container image,

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.320
you write some
Kubernetes manifest,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.110
which is how you're going
to describe your resources

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.550
on your Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.690
And then you write the
Skaffold yaml file,

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.600
which actually just
brings both of those

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.930
together and gives you the
full metadata from source

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:38.990
to deployment.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.330
So Skaffold takes that Skaffold
yaml, it will build your image,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.270
it will deploy it.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.670
It will actually introspect your
image for all of your source

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.150
code dependencies and
perform a file watch on that,

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.310
so when those files
change, it kicks off

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.650
that rapid development
loop and you're

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:56.822
able to get that native
experience, but on a cluster.

00:01:56.822 --> 00:01:59.030
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And then
you're cooking with grease.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.100
MATT RICKARD: Then you're
cooking with grease.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:00.460
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: All right.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.080
I understand you
have a demo for us.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.059
You want to walk us
through this demo?

00:02:04.059 --> 00:02:04.850
MATT RICKARD: Sure.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.558
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: All
right, let's see it.

00:02:06.558 --> 00:02:07.794
MATT RICKARD: Cool.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:12.460
So here, I'm just in the Getting
Started portion of the Skaffold

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.210
repository.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:17.110
You can check this out at
googlecontainertools/skaffold.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:23.050
And what I have here is I have
a very basic go program here.

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:25.900
It's just going to print
"Hello world!" every second.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.580
I have a Dockerfile
that describes it.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.180
So here I'm using a
multistage Dockerfile.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.820
You could really use any sort of
Dockerfile here that you want.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.870
And then I have a very simple
Kubernetes resource definition

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.210
here, which is just
a pod that deploys

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.610
this Getting Started
container on my cluster.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.700
If we look a little bit
at the Skaffold yaml,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.420
you can see two main parts
to it, the first being build.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:50.920
And we just have a
list of artifacts

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.641
here, the first being
our Skaffold example.

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.140
The really cool
thing about Skaffold

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.740
is that you can deploy multiple
images and multiple deployments

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.530
at the same time.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.930
So the idea behind that is that
in this microservice world,

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.260
cloud-native world, your
services might be split up

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.030
into many different
microservices

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.820
and you'll need to be
developing on multiple

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.460
of those at the same time.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.900
So Skaffold supports all
sorts of workflows like that.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.490
And then the deploy stanza
here is basically just saying,

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:25.540
take all of my Kubernetes
manifests and deploy them.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.052
And I'll just go ahead and
I'll do a Skaffold dev.

00:03:28.052 --> 00:03:30.010
So this is just going to
do a build and deploy,

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:34.195
and then perform a watch on
the file system dependencies.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.760
So you can see that
we're streaming

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.960
