deployed artifacts here.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.330
It's just saying, "Hello world!"

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.299
every second.

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:45.090
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Now,
that deployed locally

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.819
or that deployed to the cloud?

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:48.360
MATT RICKARD: Yeah,
so good question.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.490
So here I'm using
a Minikube cluster,

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.870
so it's local to my laptop.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.410
But Skaffold works both
remotely and on local clusters.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.420
And there are some
optimizations that we

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.800
make for the local
cases where we

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.320
don't have to push all
the artifacts around,

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.470
but the experience
is exactly the same.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.980
So now I'm just going to go
and change some code here.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.100
I'm going to say,
"Hello KubeCon!"

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.630
And then I just saved it.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:16.410
And this will kick off the
build and deploy cycle again.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.670
And you can see that we've
already redeployed our new pod,

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.750
and it's printing
out, "Hello KubeCon!"

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.860
So you get that really
fast inner development

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.030
loop for your workflow.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:30.850
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That was
incredibly, incredibly fast.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:33.550
MATT RICKARD: So of course,
this is a minimal example.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:36.150
So we're only deploying
a very small go binary.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.952
But in practice,
it is quite fast.

00:04:38.952 --> 00:04:40.410
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And have you heard

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.170
from users using Skaffold
for much larger, maybe

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.329
real production-type workloads?

00:04:45.329 --> 00:04:46.620
MATT RICKARD: Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.620
And in that case,
it's still great

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.530
for that inner development
loop, but we also

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.920
have a mode called Skaffold Run.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.630
And so Skaffold Run
will just run this

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.100
build and deploy pipeline once.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.290
So the idea is that
you can call this

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.860
from a product such as Google
Container Builder, or Jenkins,

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:06.900
or Spinnaker.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.230
And then you can have the
same kind of CI/CD workflow

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.490
as you have locally.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.320
So that bridges
the gap, and users

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.110
aren't left on this cliff
of having all of these tools

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.060
locally, and then having
to learn something

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.279
